# Git: commit & push after requested updates

When the user asks you to implement, fix, or change code in this repo:

1. Complete the requested work first (build/lint if the change is non-trivial).
2. Unless the user explicitly says **not** to commit (e.g. "بدون commit", "don't push"), run **commit + push to `origin/main`** when there are staged-worthy changes.
3. Follow the repo git safety protocol: no `--no-verify`, no force-push to `main`, no amending pushed commits unless allowed.
4. Use a concise commit message focused on **why** (1–2 sentences), via HEREDOC.
5. After push, confirm the commit hash and that `origin/main` is up to date.

Skip commit/push when:
- The user only asked a question or review (no code changes).
- There are no file changes to commit.
- The user explicitly forbids committing.

Remote: `https://github.com/raddadalmajdi/mastara-app.git` (branch `main`).
